  "body": "Found during the work on #2394 (example case turning/cone-thread).\n\n## What happens\n\nThe Profile Rough and Profile Finish rides contain runs of moves that are shorter than the output resolution of the post. The Fanuc lathe post writes them as identical consecutive `G01` blocks.\n\nReading: `examples/catalog/turning/cone-thread/cone-thread.nc` (PR for #2394). The program has 1197 lines. 408 of them are a `G01` block that is identical to the block before it. Example, from the finish ride where the nose comes off the collar corner onto the shoulder face:\n\n```\nG01 X28.404 Z20. F0.0333\nG01 X28.402 Z20. F0.0333\nG01 X28.402 Z20. F0.0333\nG01 X28.402 Z20. F0.0333\nG01 X28.4 Z20. F0.0333\nG01 X28.4 Z20. F0.0333\n(23 more identical blocks)\nG01 X19.812 Z20. F0.0333\n```\n\n## Cause\n\n`crates/motion/src/turning/offset.rs`, `profile_offset`: the chord bisection (`settle`) halves the interval toward a kink of the offset curve, where an arc meets a line or a vertical. The samples come closer than 1 micrometre. `out.dedup()` removes only samples that are exactly equal.\n\n## Effect\n\n- The program is correct in position.\n- A control can stop for a moment at each zero-length feed block. This can leave a mark on a finished face.\n- The program and the project file are larger than necessary.\n\n## What is expected\n\nThe ride has no move shorter than a small fraction of the tolerance. Alternatively the lathe post drops a feed block whose words are equal to the last position.\n\nRefs #2394\n",
